---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="about — colin vargas"
  description="Colin Vargas is a master's student in aerospace engineering focused on computational fluid dynamics, propulsion, and scientific software."
>
  <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="page-intro" aria-labelledby="about-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <span>about colin</span><span>aerospace engineering</span>
      </div><h1 id="about-title">
        drawn to the<br />hard questions<span class="accent-dot">.</span>
      </h1><div class="intro-bottom">
        <p>
          i'm a master's student in aerospace engineering, working at the
          intersection of numerical methods, fluid mechanics, and the software
          used to explore them.
        </p><p class="intro-note">
          current focus / amrex-based solver development, from scalar advection
          toward adaptive flow capability.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="about-story" aria-labelledby="thinking-title">
      <span class="eyebrow">how i think about the work</span><div>
        <h2 id="thinking-title">understand it.<br />then build on it.</h2><p>
          the most interesting questions usually sit between the physics and the
          implementation. what does a discretization assume? what does a
          boundary condition change? what would a simple case tell us before a
          larger simulation?
        </p><p>
          my thesis work gives those questions a practical home in solver
          development. propulsion analysis and numerical flow modeling bring the
          same habits into different engineering problems.
        </p><a class="text-link" href="/work"
          >explore the projects <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="portfolio-section" aria-labelledby="principles-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2 id="principles-title">working principles</h2><span
          >the habits behind the projects</span
        >
      </div><ol class="project-approach">
        <li>
          <span class="eyebrow">01 / small cases</span><h3>
            keep the first test simple.
          </h3><p>
            a tractable case makes it easier to separate the numerical behavior
            from implementation mistakes.
          </p>
        </li><li>
          <span class="eyebrow">02 / clear assumptions</span><h3>
            show what the model assumes.
          </h3><p>
            an answer becomes more useful when its physical and numerical limits
            are visible.
          </p>
        </li><li>
          <span class="eyebrow">03 / useful records</span><h3>
            make the reasoning reusable.
          </h3><p>
            the setup, inputs, figures, and code context matter as much as the
            final plot.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>
    <section class="beyond-grid about-beyond">
      <h2>curiosity doesn't<br />stop at the desk.</h2><div>
        <p>
          i also spend time learning to fly and building out homelab systems.
          they offer a different way to think about reliability,
          instrumentation, and how a system behaves in practice.
        </p><a class="text-link" href="/systems"
          >the systems side <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="page-next">
      <span class="eyebrow"
        >bring a question, a project, or an engineering problem</span
      ><a href="/contact"
        >let's compare notes. <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      >
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
